fine. Many youtube_dl tests do exactly this. Actually, hmm, in Python 3.9+:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is None:\n            fp = io.BytesIO()\n        super().__init__(url, hdrs, fp)\n        self._init_url = url  # ?\n```\n\nHmm, addinfourl.__init__(self, url, headers, fp) - wait, signature is addinfourl(fp, headers, url, code=None). Let me not worry; HTTPError construction with these args is standard and works. Actually let me double check: `urllib.error.HTTPError('http://x', 404, 'Not Found', None, None)` - I've done this many times; it works. In CPython 3:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    \"\"\"Raised when HTTP error occurs, but also acts like non-error return\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is None:\n            fp = io.BytesIO()\n        super().__init__(url, hdrs, fp)  # addinfourl.__init__(self, url, headers, fp)?\n```\n\nHmm, addinfourl signature: `addinfourl(fp, headers, url, code=None)`. But HTTPError calls... let me recall actual CPython source (Lib/urllib/error.py):\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    \"\"\"Raised when HTTP error occurs, but also acts like non-error return\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is None:\n            fp = io.BytesIO()\n        super().__init__(url, hdrs, fp)\n```\n\nHmm wait, that doesn't match addinfourl's signature. Let me check addinfourl:\n\n```python\nclass addinfourl(tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper?) \n```\n\nNo. urllib/response.py:\n\n```python\nclass addbase(tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper):\n    ...\nclass addclosehook(addbase):\n    ...\nclass addinfourl(addbase):\n    def __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None):\n        ...\n```\n\nAnd error.py:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is None:\n            fp = io.BytesIO()\n        super().__init__(url, hdrs, fp)\n```\n\nHmm, that would call addinfourl.__init__(url, hdrs, fp) which maps to (fp=url, headers=hdrs, url=fp)? That seems wrong... Let me actually recall. CPython Lib/urllib/error.py:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    \"\"\"Raised when HTTP error occurs, but also acts like non-error return\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is None:\n            fp = io.BytesIO()\n        super().__init__(url, hdrs, fp)\n```\n\nHmm no. I think it's:\n\n```python\n        super().__init__(fp, hdrs, url)\n```\n\nYes! That's it: addinfourl.__init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None). So HTTPError calls super().__init__(fp, hdrs, url). And addbase.__init__ calls tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper? No...\n\naddbase:\n\n```python\nclass addbase(tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper):\n    \"\"\"Base class for addinfo and addclosehook.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, fp, **kwds):\n        super().__init__(fp, **kwds)  # hmm\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\nclass addbase(tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper):\n    def __init__(self, fp, **kwds):\n        super(addbase, self).__init__(fp, **kwds)\n```\n\nHmm, tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper.__init__(self, file, name, delete=True). This is getting complicated