arams = params or {}\n    self._progress_hooks = []\n    self._tempfiles = []\n```\n\nHmm, I think _tempfiles was added later. Let me just check via get_info.\n\nAlso, FragmentFD:\n\n```python\nclass FragmentFD(FileDownloader):\n    def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n        FileDownloader.__init__(self, ydl, params)\n```\n\nHmm, maybe not even defined. Let me check.\n\nAlso DashSegmentsFD:\n\n```python\nclass DashSegmentsFD(FragmentFD):\n    \"\"\"\n    Download segments in DASH. Until 3.8.0, this only used the first\n    segment, which is used specifically for WebM DASH.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    FD_NAME = 'dashsegments'\n\n    def real_download(self, filename, info_dict):\n        ...\n```\n\nOK. Let me now also double check `_download_fragment` signature in this version: `def _download_fragment(self, ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None, request_data=None)`. The dash code calls `self._download_fragment(ctx, fragment_url, info_dict)` - fine.\n\nNow, one important detail: in newer youtube_dl versions, `_download_fragment` catches HTTPError internally and returns (False, None)? Let me check the actual code. From youtube-dl master (fragment.py):\n\n```python\ndef _download_fragment(self, ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None, request_data=None):\n    downloade...\n```\n\nActually from youtube-dl:\n\n```python\ndef _download_fragment(self, ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None, request_data=None):\n    success = False\n    try:\n        request = self.ydl.urlopen(\n            self._prepare_url(info_dict, frag_url, request_data, headers))\n        ctx['fragment_filename_sanitized'] = ...\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me recall the actual youtube-dl fragment.py:\n\n```python\nclass FragmentFD(FileDownloader):\n    ...\n    def _download_fragment(self, ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None, request_data=None):\n        downloade...\n```\n\nI really should just call get_info. Let me do that.\n\nPlan of get_info calls (all independent, one block):\n- 'DashSegmentsFD'\n- 'FragmentFD'\n- 'FragmentFD._download_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download'\n- 'FragmentFD._append_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD._finish_frag_download'\n- 'FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD.report_skip_fragment'\n- 'FileDownloader.__init__'\n- 'FileDownloader.report_error'\n- 'urljoin'\n\nThat's a lot but fine.\n\nThen write the test file.\n\nTest file structure:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom unittest.mock import MagicMock, call\n\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.dash import DashSegmentsFD\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import DownloadError\n\n\ndef _http_error(url='http://example.com/frag', code=404):\n    return compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, code, 'Not Found', None, None)\n\n\ndef _make_fd(params=None):\n    ydl = MagicMock()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params=params or {})\n    fd._prepare_and_start_frag_download = MagicMock(\n        side_effect=lambda ctx: ctx.update(fragment_index=0))\n    fd._download_fragment = MagicMock(return_value=(True, b'frag-data'))\n    fd._append_fragment = MagicMock()\n    fd._finish_frag_download = MagicMock()\n    fd.report_retry_fragment = MagicMock()\n    fd.report_skip_fragment = MagicMock()\n    fd.report_error = MagicMock()\n    return fd\n\n\ndef test_successful_download_all_fragments():\n    fd = _make_fd()\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg0'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'},\n        ],\n    }\n    assert fd.real_download('video.mp4', info_dict) is True\n    assert fd._download_fragment.call_count == 3\n    assert fd._download_fragment.call_args_list == [\n        call(fd.ctx..., 'http://example.com/seg0', info_dict), ...]\n```\n\nHmm, ctx is created inside real_download; we can't easily reference it. But we can capture ctx via the side_effect of _prepare_and_start_frag_download. Let's store it:\n\n```python\ndef _make_fd(params=None, fragment_index=0):\n    ydl = MagicMock()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params=params or {})\n    captured = {}\n\n    def prepare(ctx):\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = fragment_index\n        captured['ctx'] = ctx\n\n    fd._prepare_and_start_frag_download = MagicMock(side_effect=prepare)\n    ...\n    fd._captured_ctx = captured\n    return fd\n```\n\nThen assertions can check ctx['total_frags'] etc.\n\nTest 1: success path\n- 3 fragments with urls\n- assert result True\n- assert _download_fragment called 3 times with correct urls\n- assert _append_fragment called 3 times with b'frag-data'\n- assert _finish_frag_download called once with ctx\n- assert ctx['total_frags'] == 3\n\nTest 2: test mode\n- params={'test': True}\n- 3 fragments\n- assert result True\n- assert _download_fragment called once\n- assert ctx['total_frags'] == 1\n\nTest 3: path-based URL\n- info_dict = {'fragment_base_url': 'http://example.com/base/', 'fragments': [{'path': 'seg0.m4s'}, {'path': 'seg1.m4s'}]}\n- assert _download_fragment called with 'http://example.com/base/seg0.m4s' and 'http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s'\n- urljoin('http://example.com/base/', 'seg0.m4s') = 'http://example.com/base/seg0.m4s'. Yes.\n\nTest 4: resume skip\n- fragment_index=1\n- 2 fragments\n- assert _download_fragment called once, with second fragment's url\n- assert result True\n\nTest 5: HTTPError then success (retry)\n- params={'fragment_retries': 3}\n- 1 fragment\n- _download_fragment side_effect: [raise http_error, (True, b'data')]\n- assert result True\n- assert report_retry_fragment called once with (err, 1, 1, 3)\n- assert _append_fragment called once\n\nTest 6: HTTPError exhausted, fatal (first fragment)\n- params={'fragment_retries': 1}\n- 1 fragment\n- _download_fragment always raises http_error\n- assert result False\n- assert report_error called with 'giving up after 1 fragment retries'\n- assert _finish_frag_download not called\n- assert report_retry_fragment called once (count=1 <= 1)\n\nWait: count starts 0. while count <= 1: iteration 1: count=0, raise \u2192 count=1, 1<=1 \u2192 report_retry(err, 1, 1, 1). Iteration 2: count=1 <= 1: raise \u2192 count=2, 2<=1 false \u2192 no report. Loop check: count=2 > 1 \u2192 exit. Then count(2) > fragment_retries(1) \u2192 fatal (i==0) \u2192 report_error, return False. Good.\n\nTest 7: HTTPError exhausted, not fatal \u2192 skip and continue\n- params={'fragment_retries': 0} (default skip_unavailable_fragments=True)\n- 2 fragments: first succeeds, second always raises HTTPError\n- assert result True\n- assert report_skip_fragment called once with 2\n- assert _finish_frag_download called\n- assert _download_fragment call count: 1 (first) + 1 (second, one attempt) = 2\n\nWait for second fragment: count=0, while 0<=0: try \u2192 HTTPError \u2192 count=1 \u2192 1<=0 false \u2192 no report_retry. Loop: count=1 > 0 \u2192 exit while. count(1) > 0 \u2192 not fatal \u2192 report_skip_fragment(2), continue. Then loop ends (no more fragments) \u2192 _finish, return True. Good.\n\nTest 8: DownloadError not fatal \u2192 skip, break, continue with next fragment\n- params={} (skip_unavailable_fragments default True)\n- 3 fragments: first succeeds, second raises DownloadError, third succeeds\n- assert result True\n- assert report_skip_fragment called once with 2\n- assert _download_fragment called 3 times (frag1, frag2 attempt, frag3)\n- assert _finish called\n\nTest 9: DownloadError fatal \u2192 raise\n- 1 fragment raising DownloadError\n- params={'skip_unavailable_fragments': True}\n- i=0 \u2192 fatal=True \u2192 raise\n- with pytest.raises(DownloadError)\n- assert _finish not called\n\nAlso test DownloadError fatal via skip_unavailable_fragments=False on a later fragment:\n- params={'skip_unavailable_fragments': False}\n- 2 fragments: first succeeds, second raises DownloadError \u2192 fatal = (i==1)==False or not False==True \u2192 True \u2192 raise.\n- This covers the `not skip_unavailable_fragments` branch of line 40 and the raise branch again. Good for branch coverage of line 40.\n\nTest 10: success=False \u2192 return False\n- _download_fragment returns (False, None)\n- assert result False\n- assert _append_fragment not called\n- assert _finish not called\n\nTest 11: missing fragment_base_url with path-only fragment \u2192 AssertionError\n- info_dict = {'fragments': [{'path': 'seg0'}]} (no fragment_base_url)\n- with pytest.raises(AssertionError)\n- Hmm, but wait: `assert fragment_base_url` - if fragment_base_url is None, AssertionError. Yes.\n\nAlso, maybe test that HTTPError on first fragment with retries eventually succeeds... covered by test 5.\n\nAlso branch: line 61 `if count <= fragment_retries` - both branches covered by tests 5 (true) and 6/7 (false).\n\nLine 36 `if frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']` - both branches covered (test 4 true, others false).\n\nLine 40: fatal both True and False covered.\n\nLine 45 `if not fragment_url`: both branches (test 1 url present, test 3 absent).\n\nLine 49 `if not success`: both (test 10 true, others false).\n\nLine 66 `if not fatal`: both (test 8 true, test 9 false).\n\nLine 71 `if count > fragment_retries`: both (tests 6/7 true, others false).\n\nLine 72 `if not fatal`: both (test 7 true, test 6 false).\n\nLine 20-21: test param both True and False.\n\nGreat, full branch coverage of real_download.\n\nNow, about cleanup/state pollution: We're using MagicMock for ydl and patching instance attributes directly (not class attributes), so no class-level pollution. Instance objects are garbage collected after test. No files created since we patch _prepare_and_start_frag_download, _append_fragment, _finish_frag_download. No network since _download_fragment patched. Good.\n\nBut wait: is there any risk that constructing DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params) does something? FileDownloader.__init__ - let me check. In youtube_dl:\n\n```python\nclass FileDownloader(object):\n    _TESTS = {}\n\n    def __init__(self, ydl, params=None):\n        \"\"\"Create a FileDownloader object.\"\"\"\n        self.ydl = ydl\n        self.params = params or {}\n        self._progress_hooks = []\n        self._tempfiles = []\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure about _tempfiles. Actually I recall:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, ydl, params=None):\n    \"\"\"Create a FileDownloader object.\"\"\"\n    self.ydl = ydl\n    self.params = params or {}\n    self._progress_hooks = []\n    self._tempfiles = []\n```\n\nHmm, in youtube-dl's current FileDownloader:\n\n```python\nclass FileDownloader(object):\n    _TESTS = {}\n\n    def __init__(self, ydl, params=None):\n        \"\"\"Create a FileDownloader object.\"\"\"\n        self.ydl = ydl\n        self.params = params or {}\n        self._progress_hooks = []\n        self._tempfiles = []\n```\n\nI'll verify with get_info('FileDownloader.__init__').\n\nAlso `report_error` - it's defined on FileDownloader? Actually `report_error` is on YoutubeDL: `self.ydl.report_error`. FileDownloader has:\n\n```python\ndef report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n    self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, actually FileDownloader has `to_screen`, `report_warning` etc. that delegate to self.ydl. Let me check 'FileDownloader.report_error' via get_info.\n\nSince we patch report_error on the instance, it doesn't matter, but I want to confirm the attribute exists to patch (we're just setting instance attributes, so it works regardless).\n\nOne more consideration: `real_download` uses `self.params.get('test', False)` etc. Our params dict is passed to constructor. Good.\n\nAlso note: the code at line 20-21: `info_dict['fragments'][:1] if self.params.get('test', False) else info_dict['fragments']`. With test=True and 3 fragments, only first is used. total_frags=1.\n\nNow about the HTTPError object: `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. Creating with hdrs=None, fp=None. When report_retry_fragment is patched, err is just passed through. Fine.\n\nBut careful: HTTPError is a subclass of URLError and also of OSError... and it's caught by `except compat_urllib_error.HTTPError`. DownloadError is from youtube_dl.utils and is not an OSError subclass (it's a YoutubeDLError). So raising DownloadError from _download_fragment mock will be caught by the second except. Good.\n\nWait, one subtlety: in test 5, side_effect list: first call raises HTTPError, second returns (True, b'data'). With MagicMock(side_effect=[err, (True, b'data')]) - exceptions in side_effect list are raised. Good.\n\nNow, let me also double-check the `frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']` logic: ctx['fragment_index'] is set by our patched prepare. In real code, _start_frag_download updates ctx['fragment_index'] as fragments are appended. Since we patch, we control it.\n\nLet me also think about whether to use `monkeypatch` instead of directly assigning instance attributes. Directly assigning to the instance is fine and automatically cleaned up when the instance is discarded. But the instructions say \"use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate\". Assigning instance attributes on a locally-created object is inherently clean. I could use monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', MagicMock(...)) for extra safety. That's fine too. I'll use monkeypatch.setattr for patching instance methods - it's clean and explicit.\n\nActually, since fd is created inside the test function, plain assignment is equally safe. But to follow the instruction spirit, I'll use monkeypatch.setattr. Hmm, monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'name', value) works on objects. Let me do that.\n\nAlternatively, use mocker (pytest-mock) - but monkeypatch is builtin. I'll use monkeypatch.\n\nLet me write a helper that creates the fd and patches via monkeypatch:\n\n```python\ndef _make_fd(monkeypatch, params=None, fragment_index=0):\n    ydl = MagicMock()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params=params or {})\n    ctx_holder = {}\n\n    def fake_prepare(ctx):\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = fragment_index\n        ctx_holder['ctx'] = ctx\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', MagicMock(side_effect=fake_prepare))\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', MagicMock(return_value=(True, b'frag-data')))\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_append_fragment', MagicMock())\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_finish_frag_download', MagicMock())\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_retry_fragment', MagicMock())\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_skip_fragment', MagicMock())\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_error', MagicMock())\n    fd._ctx_holder = ctx_holder\n    return fd\n```\n\nHmm, monkeypatch.setattr on an instance attribute that doesn't exist on the class... monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) requires the attribute to exist? Actually monkeypatch.setattr works if the attribute exists on the object or its class; if it doesn't exist at all, it raises AttributeError unless raising=False. All these methods exist on FragmentFD/FileDownloader classes, so setattr on the instance will find them via the class. Good.\n\nWait, does `report_error` exist on FileDownloader? Let me check. In youtube_dl/downloader/common.py? FileDownloader is in youtube_dl/downloader/common.py? No - FileDownloader is in youtube_dl/downloader/common.py? Let me think: youtube_dl/downloader/common.py contains FileDownloader. Yes:\n\n```python\nclass FileDownloader(object):\n    ...\n    def report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n        self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nHmm, actually I think FileDownloader has:\n\n```python\ndef to_screen(self, *args, **kargs):\n    self.ydl.to_screen(*args, **kargs)\n\ndef to_stderr(self, message):\n    self.ydl.to_screen(message)\n\ndef report_warning(self, *args, **kargs):\n    self.ydl.report_warning(*args, **kargs)\n\ndef report_error(self, *args, **kargs):\n    self.ydl.report_error(*args, **kargs)\n```\n\nYes, something like that. I'll verify with get_info.\n\nNow, the tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_download_success_all_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    fd = _make_fd(monkeypatch)\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg0.m4s'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1.m4s'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2.m4s'},\n        ],\n    }\n    result = fd.real_download('out.mp4', info_dict)\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd._download_fragment.call_count == 3\n    assert [c.args[1] for c in fd._download_fragment.call_args_list] == [\n        'http://example.com/seg0.m4s',\n        'http://example.com/seg1.m4s',\n        'http://example.com/seg2.m4s',\n    ]\n    assert fd._append_fragment.call_count == 3\n    assert all(c.args[1] == b'frag-data' for c in fd._append_fragment.call_args_list)\n    fd._finish_frag_download.assert_called_once()\n    ctx = fd._ctx_holder['ctx']\n    assert ctx['total_frags'] == 3\n    assert ctx['filename'] == 'out.mp4'\n    fd._finish_frag_download.assert_called_once_with(ctx)\n```\n\nWait, _append_fragment(ctx, frag_content) - args[0] is ctx, args[1] is content. Good.\n\n_download_fragment(ctx, fragment_url, info_dict) - args[1] is url. Good.\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_downloads_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    fd = _make_fd(monkeypatch, params={'test': True})\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg0.m4s'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1.m4s'},\n        ],\n    }\n    result = fd.real_download('out.mp4', info_dict)\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd._ctx_holder['ctx']['total_frags'] == 1\n    assert fd._download_fragment.call_count == 1\n    assert fd._download_fragment.call_args.args[1] == 'http://example.com/seg0.m4s'\n    fd._finish_frag_download.assert_called_once()\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_fragment_url_from_base_url_and_path(monkeypatch):\n    fd = _make_fd(monkeypatch)\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragment_base_url': 'http://example.com/base/',\n        'fragments': [{'path': 'seg0.m4s'}, {'path': 'seg1.m4s'}],\n    }\n    result = fd.real_download('out.mp4', info_dict)\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert [c.args[1] for c in fd._download_fragment.call_args_list] == [\n        'http://example.com/base/seg0.m4s',\n        'http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s',\n    ]\n```\n\nurljoin('http://example.com/base/', 'seg0.m4s') \u2192 'http://example.com/base/seg0.m4s'. Yes, since base ends with '/', join appends.\n\n```python\ndef test_resume_skips_already_downloaded_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    fd = _make_fd(monkeypatch, fragment_index=1)\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg0.m4s'},\n            {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1.m4s'},\n        ],\n    }\n    result = fd.real_download('out.mp4', info_dict)\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd._download_fragment.call_count == 1\n    assert fd._download_fragment.call_args.args[1] == 'http://example.com/seg1.m4s'\n    fd._append_fragment.assert_called_once()\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retried_then_success(monkeypatch):\n    err = _http_error()\n    fd = _make_fd(monkeypatch, params={'fragment_retries': 3})\n    fd._download_fragment = ... \n```\n\nHmm, with monkeypatch I need to re-setattr. Let me restructure: allow _make_fd to accept download side effects.\n\n```python\ndef _make_fd(monkeypatch, params=None, fragment_index=0, download_results=None):\n    ...\n    dl_mock = MagicMock(return_value=(True, b'frag-data'))\n    if download_results is not None:\n        dl_mock.side_effect = download_results\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', dl_mock)\n    ...\n```\n\ndownload_results can be a list mixing exceptions and tuples.\n\nTest 5:\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retried_then_success(monkeypatch):\n    err = _http_error()\n    fd = _make_fd(\n        monkeypatch,\n        params={'fragment_retries': 3},\n        download_results=[err, (True, b'frag-data')],\n    )\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg0.m4s'}]}\n    result = fd.real_download('out.mp