pd):\n    ...\n```\n\nand they run real HTTP servers. Not helpful.\n\nI know that `requests` library tests construct HTTPError? Not sure.\n\nOK, decisive approach: I'll construct the HTTPError with all five args where fp is a real io.BytesIO() and hdrs is an email.message.Message() or {}. That covers all bases:\n\n```python\nimport io\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error\n\ndef _http_error(code=404):\n    return compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(\n        'http://example.com/frag', code, 'Not Found', {}, io.BytesIO(b''))\n```\n\nHmm, but if hdrs must support .items()... nothing touches it. With fp=BytesIO, the `if fp is None` branch is skipped, and addinfourl gets a real file-like. hdrs={} \u2014 addinfourl stores headers; fine.\n\nActually wait \u2014 in Python 3, addinfourl.__init__ does:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None):\n    if isinstance(headers, email.message.Message):\n        headers = ...\n```\n\nno. Real:\n\n```python\nclass addinfourl(addbase):\n    \"\"\"Class to add info and geturl methods to an open file.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None):\n        if isinstance(headers, email.message.Message):\n            headers = email.message.Message  # NO\n```\n\nI clearly can't recall. {} as headers is safe either way since it's just stored (maybe .items() called lazily on info()).\n\nHmm, but actually there's one more consideration: in Python 3.3+, addbase wraps fp with tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper? No \u2014 addbase:\n\n```python\nclass addbase(tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper):\n    \"\"\"Base class for addinfo and addclosehook.\"\"\"\n```\n\nWait, that IS the actual definition in Python 3! addbase subclasses _TemporaryFileWrapper so that HTTPError instances can be used as context managers. And _TemporaryFileWrapper.__init__(self, file, name, delete=True). So addinfourl.__init__(fp, headers, url, code) must call super().__init__(fp, url) or similar... \n\nPython 3.8 urllib/response.py (actual):